 "answer": "**Part A**\nWe want the sum of integers $x \\in [675, 8626]$ such that $13 \\mid x$ and $9 \\nmid x$.\nFirst, find integers divisible by 13:\n$\\lceil 675 / 13 \\rceil = \\lceil 51.923 \\rceil = 52$.\n$\\lfloor 8626 / 13 \\rfloor = 663$ (since $13 \\times 663 = 8619$).\nSum of multiples of 13:\n$S_{13} = 13 \\times \\sum_{k=52}^{663} k = 13 \\times \\frac{(52 + 663) \\times (663 - 52 + 1)}{2} = 13 \\times \\frac{715 \\times 612}{2} = 13 \\times 715 \\times 306 = 2,844,270$.\n\nNow, multiples of $\\operatorname{lcm}(13, 9) = 117$:\n$\\lceil 675 / 117 \\rceil = \\lceil 5.769 \\rceil = 6$.\n$\\lfloor 8626 / 117 \\rfloor = 73$ (since $117 \\times 73 = 8541$).\nSum of multiples of 117:\n$S_{117} = 117 \\times \\sum_{k=6}^{73} k = 117 \\times \\frac{(6 + 73) \\times (73 - 6 + 1)}{2} = 117 \\times \\frac{79 \\times 68}{2} = 117 \\times 79 \\times 34 = 314,262$.\n\nSubtract to find the sum:\n$S = S_{13} - S_{117} = 2,844,270 - 314,262 = 2,530,008$.\n\n---\n\n**Part B**\nWe need the smallest positive integer $x$ such that:\n$x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{19}$\n$x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{13}$\n$x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{16}$\n\nFrom $x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{19}$ and $x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{16}$:\nSince $\\gcd(19, 16) = 1$, $x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{19 \\times 16} \\implies x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{304}$.\nSo $x = 304k + 11$.\nModulo 13:\n$304 = 23 \\times 13 + 5 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{13}$.\nSo:\n$5k + 11 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{13}$\n$5k \\equiv -7 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{13}$\nMultiply by 8 (since $5 \\times 8 = 40 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}$):\n$k \\equiv 48 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{13}$.\n\nThe smallest non-negative integer is $k = 9$:\n$x = 304(9) + 11 = 2736 + 11 = 2747$.\nCheck:\n$2747 / 19 = 144$ R $11$.\n$2747 / 13 = 211$ R $4$.\n$2747 / 16 = 171$ R $11$.\nThus, $x = 2747$.\n\n---\n\n**Part C**\nEach period:\nBalance $B_{n} = (B_{n-1} + C_n) \\times 1.015 \\times (1 - 0.0075) = (B_{n-1} + C_n) \\times 1.015 \\times 0.9925 = (B_{n-1} + C_n) \\times 1.0073875$.\nLet $r = 1.0073875$.\nStart with $B_0 = 5000$.\n- Period 1: $C_1 = 0